 {"attempt_id": "C0016-A001", "author": "claude-probability-1000-continue-0907", "independent_check": "", "key_identity": "", "method": "Compare with known dimensions of LQG geodesics and of the LQG metric ball boundary.", "obstruction": "Continuum object not defined; only the discrete energy scaling is accessible.", "problem_id": "C0016", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T06:38:45Z", "result": "If the continuum path were an LQG geodesic its Euclidean Hausdorff dimension would be that of LQG geodesics, which is strictly greater than 1 and below 2 and only known within bounds for general gamma; the energy support, if concentrated on a fractal set, would have dimension between the geodesic dimension and 2. I could not do better than these bounds because the object (C0015) is not constructed. A rigorous discrete statement is available: the discrete energy of the minimising current between opposite sides of [0,N]^2 is bounded above and below by constants times the effective conductance, whose growth exponent is the same as that of the resistance in C0014, so the energy is not carried by a set of full dimension 2 whenever the resistance exponent is non-trivial.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true}
